WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.720
This is an egg.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.240
But then, you already knew that.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.440
You know because eggs have been
central to human existence

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.840
for thousands of years.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.280
In fact, you probably take it for
granted, after all, it's only an egg.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.120
But if you're prepared
to look closer -

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.360
to see through its
calcium carbonate shell -

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.840
you'll find a microcosm
of the universe.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.760
Don't believe me?
Let's start at the beginning.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.120
The very beginning.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.520
Several religions, multiple
traditions, the Ancient Egyptians,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.040
the Greeks, the Romans, the Incas

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.920
all have eggs at the heart
of their creation stories.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.360
In southern California, the origin
story of the Cahuilla people

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.880
likens the creation of the entire
universe to the cracking of an egg.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.520
A little further east, the Omaha
tribe of Nebraska and Iowa

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.280
spoke of an egg being dropped
into the world's oceans.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.280
Protected by a bird serpent,
inside this egg lay sleeping

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.520
all of the mothers and all of the
fathers of everyone yet to be born.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.560
But these are stories.
Where's the science?

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.480
In 2006, data gathered
by Nasa's Wilkinson satellite

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.120
suggested that the Universe itself
may be an ellipsoid - an oval.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.360
Egg shaped.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.360
The science community remain unable

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.800
to categorically prove or disprove
this theory but it remains possible

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.280
we're all living inside a massive,
ever-expanding egg.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.040
Delicious!

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.120
In 1609, Johannes Kepler confirmed
that the planets, including our own,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.160
go around the sun, not in a perfect
circle but in an elliptical orbit.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.080
The moon's elliptical orbit

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.040
makes it appear to regularly
change size in the night sky.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.720
Without this egg-shaped dance our
moon may seem far less interesting.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.320
Far from being smooth, an egg's shell
is more like the surface of the moon.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.840
Bumpy and grainy in texture,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.040
a single eggshell is covered
in up to 17,000 tiny craters.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.040
But it's also semi-permeable,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.200
allowing air and moisture
to pass through it.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.240
Oh dear.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.160
This remarkable shape,

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.800
not only is it possibly the shape
of the actual universe,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.080
it's also the pinnacle
of architectural design.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.800
Might be able to get
parts by Thursday.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.480
Here is a structure
with no internal solid support

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.160
and yet it's strong enough to protect
and nurture life itself inside.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.360
If one point of the shell
is put under external force,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.000
the stress is distributed
evenly across the entire dome.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.760
Which is why you can't crush an egg
with the palm of your hand.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.040
But how to replicate nature's
perfect aerodynamic design?

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.400
An egg has no obvious beginning
or end so where do you even start?

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.080
It wasn't until the 20th Century

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.880
that human architects
started to get the hang

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.280
of building egg-like structures
on a grand scale.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.680
'The Egg' in Beijing
seats 5,452 people

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.360
in three halls and is more than
1,000 square metres in size.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.880
That's one seriously big egg.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.360
Imagine the size of the egg cup.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.240
Eggs have inspired artists
from Dali to Faberge

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.880
and made memorable movie cameos -

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.320
Sigourney Weaver's haunted
12-pack in Ghostbusters,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.240
Paul Newman eating 50
in Cool Hand Luke,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.160
and John Hurt falling foul of a
particularly nasty one in Alien.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.280
In Gulliver's Travels,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.640
when the people of Lilliput
went to war with their neighbours,

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.200
it was over which way round
to eat a boiled egg.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.360
Could somebody please untie me?

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.840
Uh, we could do it Thursday.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.760
On Instagram, we liked this egg,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.080
in greater numbers than we'd liked
anything else ever before.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.560
Mr Strong ate nothing but eggs,
which actually was not a bad idea.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.760
An egg's protein has the perfect mix
of amino acids

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.840
required to build human tissue,
second only to our mother's milk.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.840
Eggs have also whisked their
way into our everyday lexicon.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.200
I'm not yolking. Let me eggsplain -

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.040
people can be 'good eggs'
or 'bad eggs'.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.120
If you're particularly smart -

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.800
an eggspert in your
chosen field perhaps -

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.080
you might be called an 'egghead'.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.360
We send our kids on egg hunts.
We scramble, we poach, we bake.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.560
If you want to make an omelette,
we all know what to do.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.040
Not knowing how to boil an egg

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.200
is considered shorthand
for someone who can't cook.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.440
And yet, when searching
'how to boil an egg',

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.720
Google will offer up
more than a billion results.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.280
Perhaps it's not as simple
as we thought.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.280
Much like the egg itself.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.440
People have been welcoming spring by
decorating eggs with bright colours

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.520
since the Middle Ages.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.000
But John Cadbury didn't make
his first chocolate egg until 1875.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.240
Eighty million chocolate eggs are now
sold every year in the UK alone.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.520
The average child eats eight.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.680
At Easter, it seems, the egg
will always come first...

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.720
But then of course,
you already knew that.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:27.400
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

